Linux Distributions

Today I learned about Linux distributions, package management, and file systems.

Linux Distributions

A Linux distribution is a complete operating system built using the Linux kernel.

Popular distributions include:

Ubuntu
Debian
Fedora
Arch Linux
Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL)
Red Hat

RHEL (Red Hat Enterprise Linux) is designed for enterprise environments and is known for its security, stability, and long-term support.

IBM acquired Red Hat in 2019.

Fedora & CentOS
Fedora is a free distribution sponsored by Red Hat and acts as the testing ground for RHEL.
CentOS Stream is the development branch between Fedora and RHEL.
RPM

RPM (Red Hat Package Manager) is the package format used in Red Hat-based distributions.

Unlike Windows (.exe), Linux commonly uses:

.rpm
.deb
Snap
Flatpak
AppImage
File Systems

Linux commonly uses:

ext4
XFS
Btrfs

It can also read Windows file systems like FAT32 and NTFS.

What I Learned
A Linux distribution combines the Linux kernel with software and tools.
RHEL is built for enterprise use.
Fedora is the testing ground for RHEL.
Linux uses package managers instead of .exe installers.
Modern Linux supports multiple file systems, including NTFS.
